<form action="/exercises" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data">
  <%== csrf_tag('/exercises') %>
  <div>
    <label for="name" class="block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Name</label>
    <div class="mt-2">
      <input value="<%= @exercise&.name || nil %>" name="name" id="name"
             class="<%= field_style %> block w-full py-1.5 text-gray-900 placeholder:text-gray-400 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6">
    </div>
    <%# Coaching intent for the movement itself: why it is in the program, what to
        watch for, the cue that makes it work. It belongs here rather than on a set
        because "this helps correct valgus" is true of the lift every time it is done,
        not of one Tuesday's third set. text-base is what the browser was already giving
        the inputs above by way of Tailwind's preflight, written out because a textarea
        under 16px is the size iOS Safari zooms the page in on when it takes focus, and
        the way back out is a pinch. min-h-11 is the 44px a thumb is aimed at; rows="3"
        already clears it, and the floor is here so shrinking the row count later cannot
        quietly take the tap target with it. Everything between the two tags is the
        field's value, newlines and indentation included, so the note has to butt
        straight up against both -- which is why the long class list is not the last
        attribute here as it is on the inputs above. %>
    <label for="note" class="mt-4 block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Note</label>
    <div class="mt-2">
      <textarea name="note" id="note" rows="3"
                       class="<%= field_style %> block min-h-11 w-full py-1.5 text-base text-gray-900 placeholder:text-gray-400 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6"
                placeholder="Why it is in the program, and what to watch for."><%= @exercise&.note %></textarea>
    </div>
    <%# The one thing about a movement the app cannot work out from its name once the
        movement is not one of the built-in fifty-four: whether its sets are loaded on a
        bar, and so whether they get a plate breakdown and a warmup ramp. %>
    <div class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-2">
      <input type="checkbox" name="is_barbell" id="is_barbell" value="1" <%= 'checked' if @exercise&.is_barbell %> class="h-4 w-4 rounded border-gray-300 text-lime-500 accent-lime-500 focus:ring-lime-500">
      <label for="is_barbell" class="text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Loaded on a barbell</label>
    </div>
    <%# The second thing the app cannot work out from a name, and the one that changes the
        arithmetic rather than the drawing: a movement done one limb at a time is written
        3x8 and lifted sixteen times, so counted_reps doubles it and every volume figure
        downstream depends on the answer. #279.

        The column has existed since the per-side work and every write path could set it --
        create_program, add_program_lift, the MCP exercise tools -- except the one a person
        actually uses. So a Bulgarian split squat added in the browser was half the volume
        it should have been, silently, and the only way to correct it was to ask an
        assistant to do it.

        A default rather than a fact about one set: a prescription may override it for a
        block that wants the dumbbell press done one arm at a time, which is what
        shape_of already reads. This is what that override falls back to. %>
    <div class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-2">
      <input type="checkbox" name="default_is_per_side" id="default_is_per_side" value="1"
             <%= 'checked' if @exercise&.default_is_per_side %>
             class="h-4 w-4 rounded border-gray-300 text-lime-500 accent-lime-500 focus:ring-lime-500">
      <label for="default_is_per_side" class="text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Counted per side</label>
    </div>
  </div>
  <input type="hidden" name="id" id="id" value="<%= @exercise&.id || nil %>">
  <input type="submit" value="Save" class="<%= button_style %> mt-4 block bg-lime-500 px-3 py-2 text-center text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">
</form>
